17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"July 1–4, 1862","place":"Malvern Hill in Henrico County, Virginia, southeast of Richmond, followed by the Army of the Potomac’s withdrawal along the James River to Harrison’s Landing","result":"United States victory at Malvern Hill. Federal artillery and infantry repulsed a series of disjointed Confederate attacks on July 1. George B. McClellan nevertheless withdrew to Harrison’s Landing, where naval guns and field fortifications protected the army. Robert E. Lee ended the Seven Days campaign and returned most of his army toward Richmond","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The fighting at Malvern Hill and the close of the Seven Days campaign","note":"The episode covers the failed Confederate artillery plan, the infantry assaults late on July 1, the Federal withdrawal on July 2, and Lee’s decision on July 4 to end the campaign."},{"label":"Battlefield advantage","value":"Open approaches exposed attackers to concentrated Federal fire","note":"Malvern Hill was a modest elevation about two and one-half miles north of the James River. Its military strength came from long, gently sloping fields of fire and a defensible plateau rather than steep height."},{"label":"Confederate artillery plan","value":"Two massed batteries were intended to create a crossfire","note":"Lee assigned James Longstreet to the Confederate right and Thomas J. Jackson to the left. Robert H. Chilton drafted the related infantry order, but the guns were not concentrated as planned."},{"label":"Largest Confederate concentration","value":"Sixteen guns in William H. C. Whiting’s sector","note":"The episode identifies Whiting’s three batteries in the Poindexter wheat field as the largest Confederate concentration of the day. Thirty-one Federal guns about 800 yards away answered them."},{"label":"Artillery deployment failure","value":"Batteries entered separately and were defeated in detail","note":"Jackson’s batteries began before Longstreet’s guns were ready. West of Willis Church Road, no more than eight Confederate guns were active together in the episode’s account, while 35 nearby batteries never entered the fight."},{"label":"Confederate artillery casualties","value":"Nearly 100 in the episode’s account","note":"The artillery exchange produced little effect on the Federal position and also exposed supporting Confederate infantry and horses to concentrated fire."},{"label":"Lee changes course","value":"By about 3 p.m., the original artillery plan had failed","note":"Lee and Longstreet examined the Federal right for a possible July 2 attack, but Lee did not cancel Chilton’s earlier untimed order for an assault on July 1."},{"label":"Conflicting battlefield reports","value":"Routine Federal battery relief was mistaken for withdrawal","note":"Federal batteries that had expended much of their ammunition moved back to resupply while replacements came forward. Whiting reported that Confederate fire had driven them away."},{"label":"Magruder’s report","value":"Armistead had driven off Federal sharpshooters","note":"John B. Magruder sent Capt. A. G. Dickinson to report his arrival and Lewis A. Armistead’s limited advance. The report did not establish that the main Federal line was retreating."},{"label":"Lee’s definite attack order","value":"Reached Magruder at about 5 p.m.","note":"Lee directed Magruder to advance rapidly, press forward his whole line, and follow up Armistead’s reported success. Chilton’s earlier order reached Magruder near the same time."},{"label":"Magruder’s available force","value":"About 15,000 men by his estimate","note":"Not all of these troops were aligned or ready when the orders arrived. Brigades entered the fight as they reached their assigned sectors."},{"label":"Opening infantry attacks","value":"Ambrose R. Wright and William Mahone advanced at about 5:30 p.m.","note":"Their brigades moved toward the Crew house sector, crossed open ground under artillery fire, and stopped short of the Federal line."},{"label":"Armistead and Cobb","value":"Both assaults were repulsed near the Federal gun line","note":"Armistead’s men reached a forward position near Charles Griffin’s brigade before a counterattack drove them back. Howell Cobb’s supporting attack forced some infantry behind the guns but broke under point-blank canister."},{"label":"Ames’s battery expenditure","value":"1,392 rounds of shell and canister","note":"The episode gives this total for the six guns of Battery A, 5th U.S. Artillery, commanded by Lt. Adelbert Ames west of Willis Church Road."},{"label":"D. H. Hill’s attack","value":"Five brigades entered piecemeal through broken wooded terrain","note":"Hill heard the attack near 5:30 p.m. and treated it as the signal for a general advance. Woods disrupted alignment, and the brigades did not emerge as one coordinated force."},{"label":"D. H. Hill’s division losses","value":"Nearly 2,000 casualties from about 8,000 engaged in the episode’s account","note":"The 3rd Alabama lost six color bearers during the advance. Hill later condemned the attack because his division had been sent against a strong position without coordinated support."},{"label":"Barksdale’s brigade","value":"About one-third of 1,200 men became casualties","note":"The Mississippians advanced toward the cabins and barn north of the Crew house but did not break the main Federal defense."},{"label":"McLaws’s late attack","value":"Only parts of Semmes’s and Kershaw’s brigades entered before darkness","note":"The episode states that Paul J. Semmes’s troops reached the cabin area about 50 yards from the Federal line before a counterattack drove them back."},{"label":"Closest Confederate advance","value":"The exact relationship to the Federal line is disputed","note":"The episode states that no Confederate troops penetrated the Union line. The Historic American Landscapes Survey narrative describes a brief penetration late in the battle. The schematic map shows only generalized approach sectors."},{"label":"Senior commander losses","value":"No principal general officer was killed or mortally wounded","note":"Confederate brigadier generals George B. Anderson and John R. Jones were wounded. The episode does not identify the death or mortal wounding of a senior commander as part of its narrative."},{"label":"Battle casualties","value":"About 5,355 Confederate and 3,000 United States casualties","note":"These are the rounded National Park Service totals. The episode states more than 5,000 Confederate casualties and fewer than 3,000 Federal casualties. Published totals vary by source and classification."},{"label":"Tactical result","value":"United States victory","note":"No Confederate assault secured the Malvern Hill position on July 1, and the attacking force suffered the larger loss."},{"label":"Night withdrawal","value":"McClellan ordered the army to Harrison’s Landing","note":"Fitz John Porter and other commanders favored a counterattack, but McClellan continued the movement to the James. The retreat covered roughly eight to nine miles, depending on the starting point used."},{"label":"Harrison’s Landing","value":"About 90,000 troops occupied a compact protected enclave","note":"The episode describes the remaining army, its animals, and its transport crowded into about four square miles behind field defenses and the guns of the United States Navy."},{"label":"Campaign conclusion","value":"Lee ended operations on July 4","note":"After observing that the Harrison’s Landing defenses and gunboats prevented a practical assault, Lee ordered most of the Army of Northern Virginia back toward Richmond."},{"label":"Seven Days losses","value":"More than 20,000 Confederate and about 16,000 United States casualties","note":"These are the episode’s approximate campaign totals. Lee had removed the immediate threat to Richmond, while McClellan preserved his army at the James River."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Seven Days’ Battles: The War Begins Anew” by Judkin Browning","note":"The official episode page identifies this title and author. The automated transcript renders the author’s first name as “Jedkin.”"},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Huger, McLaws, Semmes, Kearny, Haxall’s Landing, Averell, and Boatswain’s Swamp","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “UJ,” “McClaw,” “Paul Sims,” “Carney,” “Haxell’s,” “Averill,” and “Bose and Swamp.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"Richmond, Malvern Hill, and Harrison’s Landing","caption":"Malvern Hill stood southeast of Richmond and north of the James River. After the July 1 victory, the Army of the Potomac withdrew to Harrison’s Landing. Lee followed after weather delayed pursuit, inspected the protected river position, and ended the campaign on July 4. Locations, routes, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Library of Congress campaign maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep167-regional-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map locating Richmond and Malvern Hill in Virginia and showing the Union withdrawal to Harrison’s Landing on the James River."},{"title":"Generalized positions and attack sectors at Malvern Hill","caption":"Federal infantry and artillery occupied the plateau while Confederate batteries and brigades approached from the north. Jackson and Whiting operated east of Willis Church Road; Magruder directed major attacks west of the road and near the Crew house; D. H. Hill’s brigades advanced through woods farther east. The arrows do not reconstruct exact routes, timing, formations, or frontage. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, Historic American Landscapes Survey, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert Knox Sneden maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep167-malvern-hill-tactical.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical map of the Federal line on Malvern Hill and generalized Confederate artillery positions and infantry attack sectors."},{"title":"Alfred Waud’s battlefield drawing","caption":"Alfred R. Waud drew the fighting at Malvern Hill on July 1, 1862. The work records United States troops and artillery in action, but it is an eyewitness drawing rather than a surveyed map.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep167-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of United States infantry and artillery fighting at Malvern Hill on July 1, 1862."}],"moments":[{"time":"5:33","audioTime":333,"title":"The Confederate artillery plan is restated","detail":"Lee and Longstreet intend to mass guns on both sides of Willis Church Road and create a crossfire before an infantry assault."},{"time":"6:18","audioTime":378,"title":"Chilton’s order remains imprecise","detail":"Lee’s aide drafts the key instruction without a fixed time and without a later cancellation when the artillery plan fails."},{"time":"7:17","audioTime":437,"title":"Jackson sends batteries forward separately","detail":"Guns enter the Poindexter wheat field as they arrive instead of waiting for a larger concentration."},{"time":"7:34","audioTime":454,"title":"Whiting receives the order to open fire","detail":"Whiting objects to placing sixteen guns in the exposed field but carries out Jackson’s order."},{"time":"8:25","audioTime":505,"title":"Federal guns engage Whiting’s batteries","detail":"Thirty-one guns about 800 yards away strike the Confederate pieces, crews, and horses."},{"time":"9:01","audioTime":541,"title":"The planned crossfire does not develop","detail":"Jackson’s guns begin before Longstreet’s batteries are ready, and the Confederate artillery remains divided."},{"time":"10:00","audioTime":600,"title":"Unused Confederate batteries are counted","detail":"The episode states that 35 nearby batteries never enter the battle."},{"time":"11:40","audioTime":700,"title":"Lee recognizes that the artillery plan has failed","detail":"At about 3 p.m., Lee and Longstreet begin examining the Federal right for a possible attack on July 2."},{"time":"13:11","audioTime":791,"title":"The order sequence remains disputed","detail":"The episode distinguishes disagreements about when Chilton wrote the order from the established fact that Lee never cancelled it."},{"time":"15:21","audioTime":922,"title":"Magruder reaches the field","detail":"Magruder assesses Armistead’s forward position, the Federal line, and the effect of Federal artillery."},{"time":"16:11","audioTime":972,"title":"Dickinson carries Magruder’s report","detail":"The message states that Magruder has arrived and that Armistead has driven back Federal sharpshooters."},{"time":"16:29","audioTime":989,"title":"Federal batteries rotate for ammunition","detail":"Confederate observers mistake battery relief and resupply for evidence that the Federal artillery is withdrawing."},{"time":"17:30","audioTime":1050,"title":"Lee orders Magruder to advance","detail":"Lee directs Magruder to press forward his whole line based on reports that the enemy is leaving and Armistead has succeeded."},{"time":"19:06","audioTime":1146,"title":"Magruder receives Chilton’s earlier order","detail":"The untimed order arrives near Lee’s definite instruction and reinforces Magruder’s conclusion that he must attack immediately."},{"time":"20:04","audioTime":1204,"title":"Wright’s brigade begins the infantry assault","detail":"At about 5:30 p.m., Wright advances toward the Crew house under heavy Federal fire, followed by Mahone."},{"time":"21:04","audioTime":1264,"title":"Armistead attacks near Griffin’s line","detail":"Armistead reaches a forward position, exchanges fire, and is driven back by concentrated fire and a Federal counterattack."},{"time":"21:34","audioTime":1294,"title":"Cobb’s supporting charge fails","detail":"Cobb’s men push Federal infantry behind the gun line but break under point-blank canister."},{"time":"22:32","audioTime":1352,"title":"Ames’s battery expenditure is recorded","detail":"Battery A, 5th U.S. Artillery, fires 1,392 rounds of shell and canister during the battle."},{"time":"23:26","audioTime":1406,"title":"D. H. Hill commits his division","detail":"Hill hears the assault and orders his brigades forward, although the terrain prevents a coordinated advance."},{"time":"25:24","audioTime":1524,"title":"The episode rejects a Confederate penetration","detail":"The narrative states that Hill’s men did not enter the Federal line and came no closer than about 200 yards."},{"time":"25:50","audioTime":1551,"title":"Hill’s losses are summarized","detail":"The episode gives nearly 2,000 casualties among roughly 8,000 men engaged in Hill’s division."},{"time":"26:22","audioTime":1582,"title":"Barksdale attacks near the Crew house","detail":"The Mississippi brigade advances toward the cabins and barn and loses about one-third of its strength."},{"time":"27:09","audioTime":1629,"title":"McLaws’s division enters near darkness","detail":"Lee sends portions of Semmes’s and Kershaw’s brigades farther right, but fading light limits the attack."},{"time":"28:01","audioTime":1681,"title":"Battle casualties are assessed","detail":"The episode reports more than 5,000 Confederate casualties and fewer than 3,000 United States casualties."},{"time":"28:51","audioTime":1731,"title":"Lee questions Magruder","detail":"Magruder states that he attacked in response to Lee’s repeated orders."},{"time":"30:39","audioTime":1839,"title":"Averell sees the battlefield on July 2","detail":"The 3rd Pennsylvania Cavalry commander describes lines of dead and wounded on the slopes as the rear guard leaves."},{"time":"31:59","audioTime":1919,"title":"McClellan declines a counterattack","detail":"Despite advice from Porter and other commanders, McClellan orders the army to continue to Harrison’s Landing."},{"time":"34:37","audioTime":2077,"title":"Rain obstructs the withdrawal","detail":"The river road becomes mud, and troops abandon equipment and wagons during the movement."},{"time":"35:40","audioTime":2140,"title":"Lee examines Harrison’s Landing","detail":"On July 4, Lee determines that field defenses and naval gunfire make a successful assault impractical."},{"time":"36:05","audioTime":2165,"title":"The Seven Days campaign ends","detail":"Lee orders most of his army back toward Richmond after McClellan establishes a protected base on the James."},{"time":"37:51","audioTime":2271,"title":"The episode book is identified","detail":"The recommendation is Judkin Browning’s “The Seven Days’ Battles: The War Begins Anew.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Selected Harrison’s Landing as the army’s destination and ordered the Malvern Hill position abandoned after the victory"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps and principal battlefield direction","subordinates":"George W. Morell’s division, including brigades under Charles Griffin and John H. Martindale, held the western and central sectors"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Darius N. Couch","command":"Division, IV Corps","subordinates":"Held the eastern sector and received reinforcement as the Confederate attacks developed"},{"commander":"Col. Henry J. Hunt","command":"Artillery Reserve chief","subordinates":"Helped organize the concentrated batteries that dominated the open approaches"},{"commander":"Lt. Adelbert Ames","command":"Battery A, 5th U.S. Artillery","subordinates":"Six guns west of Willis Church Road; the episode credits the battery with firing 1,392 rounds"},{"commander":"Maj. Gens. Samuel P. Heintzelman and Edwin V. Sumner","command":"Reinforcing corps commanders","subordinates":"Elements of their commands moved forward to support the established line late in the battle"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Approved the artillery plan, did not cancel Chilton’s untimed order, and later directed Magruder to attack"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John B. Magruder","command":"Principal direction of the late infantry attacks","subordinates":"Committed brigades under Armistead, Wright, Mahone, Cobb, Barksdale, Semmes, and Kershaw as they became available"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Confederate left and artillery concentration east of Willis Church Road","subordinates":"William H. C. Whiting’s batteries entered the Poindexter wheat field without a massed crossfire"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Confederate right artillery assignment","subordinates":"Batteries west of Willis Church Road entered separately; Longstreet later scouted the Federal right with Lee"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Division in the Confederate center-left","subordinates":"Brigades under Robert E. Rodes, Roswell S. Ripley, Samuel Garland Jr., George B. Anderson, and Alfred H. Colquitt advanced through broken wooded terrain"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin Huger","command":"Division whose brigades entered under Magruder’s direction","subordinates":"Brigades under Lewis A. Armistead, Ambrose R. Wright, and William Mahone made major attacks"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Lafayette McLaws","command":"Division committed near dusk","subordinates":"Parts of Paul J. Semmes’s and Joseph B. Kershaw’s brigades entered the attack before darkness"},{"commander":"Col. Robert H. Chilton","command":"Assistant adjutant general on Lee’s staff","subordinates":"Drafted the untimed artillery-and-infantry order that remained in force after the artillery plan failed"}]}],"people":[{"name":"John B. Magruder","role":"Confederate division commander who organized most of the late infantry attacks after receiving two orders from Lee","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep167-magruder.jpg"},{"name":"Adelbert Ames","role":"United States artillery officer whose six-gun battery fired 1,392 rounds in the episode’s account","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep167-adelbert-ames.jpg"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander responsible for the artillery plan and the orders that sent Magruder forward","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who continued the withdrawal to Harrison’s Landing after the victory","side":"United States"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander associated with the principal Federal direction and defense on Malvern Hill","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry J. Hunt","role":"Chief of the Army of the Potomac’s artillery reserve who helped organize the Federal gun line","side":"United States"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander whose brigades attacked piecemeal and suffered nearly 2,000 casualties in the episode’s account","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate commander responsible for the artillery sector east of Willis Church Road","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William H. C. Whiting","role":"Confederate division commander who objected to placing sixteen guns in the exposed Poindexter wheat field","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Lewis A. Armistead","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose forward movement became the stated signal and justification for wider attack","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Ambrose R. Wright","role":"Confederate brigade commander who began advancing toward the Crew house sector at about 5:30 p.m.","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William Mahone","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose troops followed Wright across the exposed ground","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Charles Griffin","role":"United States brigade commander whose troops shifted to meet Armistead and counterattacked","side":"United States"},{"name":"William W. Averell","role":"3rd Pennsylvania Cavalry commander who described the battlefield while serving with the rear guard on July 2","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip Kearny","role":"United States division commander who denounced the decision to leave Malvern Hill after the victory","side":"United States"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep167-regional-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of Richmond, Malvern Hill, the James River, and Harrison’s Landing.","caption":"The Army of the Potomac moved from the Richmond approaches through Malvern Hill to a fortified base at Harrison’s Landing.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Library of Congress campaign maps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep167-malvern-hill-tactical.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the Federal line and Confederate attack sectors at Malvern Hill.","caption":"Federal artillery covered open approaches while Confederate batteries and brigades entered at different times and in separate sectors.","source":"National Park Service, Historic American Landscapes Survey, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert Knox Sneden maps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep167-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of United States infantry and artillery at Malvern Hill.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud made this drawing on July 1, 1862. Its period title identifies the Federal victory over forces led by Magruder and Jackson.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep167-malvern-hill-currier-ives.jpg","alt":"Hand-colored Currier and Ives lithograph depicting the Battle of Malvern Hill.","caption":"Currier and Ives published this hand-colored battle lithograph. It is a popular print rather than a literal reconstruction of troop positions.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep167-magruder.jpg","alt":"Full-length photographic portrait of Confederate general John B. Magruder in uniform.","caption":"John B. Magruder organized and directed most of the late Confederate infantry attacks after receiving Lee’s orders.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep167-adelbert-ames.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of United States officer Adelbert Ames.","caption":"At Malvern Hill, Lt. Adelbert Ames commanded Battery A, 5th U.S. Artillery. The episode credits its six guns with 1,392 rounds fired.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep167-harrisons-landing-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of the United States defensive line at Harrison’s Landing.","caption":"Waud drew artillery and fortifications at Harrison’s Landing in July or August 1862 after the Army of the Potomac established its James River base.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 167 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/10/09/civil-war-podcast-episode-167/"},{"label":"Richmond National Battlefield Park: The Battle of Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/mhbull.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary: Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va021"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress Historic American Landscapes Survey: Battle of Malvern Hill","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/va2114/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: revised plan of the Battle of Malvern Hill by Robert Knox Sneden","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00034/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: map of the night march from Malvern Hill to Harrison’s Landing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00096/"},{"label":"Primary visual source: Alfred R. Waud’s Malvern Hill drawing, July 1, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660457/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Currier and Ives Battle of Malvern Hill lithograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/90709064/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John B. Magruder portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2007677208/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Adelbert Ames portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666388/"},{"label":"Primary visual source: Alfred R. Waud’s Harrison’s Landing line drawing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660424/"}]}}]}]
18:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Alfred Waud’s battlefield drawing",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep167-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Alfred Waud’s battlefield drawing at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep167-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of United States infantry and artillery fighting at Malvern Hill on July 1, 1862.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Alfred Waud’s battlefield drawing"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alfred R. Waud drew the fighting at Malvern Hill on July 1, 1862. The work records United States troops and artillery in action, but it is an eyewitness drawing rather than a surveyed map."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep167-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
19:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Selected Harrison’s Landing as the army’s destination and ordered the Malvern Hill position abandoned after the victory"}]]}],["$","div","V Corps and principal battlefield direction-Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"V Corps and principal battlefield direction"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"George W. Morell’s division, including brigades under Charles Griffin and John H. Martindale, held the western and central sectors"}]]}],["$","div","Division, IV Corps-Brig. Gen. Darius N. Couch",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Division, IV Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Darius N. Couch"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Held the eastern sector and received reinforcement as the Confederate attacks developed"}]]}],["$","div","Artillery Reserve chief-Col. Henry J. Hunt",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Artillery Reserve chief"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Henry J. Hunt"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Helped organize the concentrated batteries that dominated the open approaches"}]]}],["$","div","Battery A, 5th U.S. Artillery-Lt. Adelbert Ames",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Battery A, 5th U.S. Artillery"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Lt. Adelbert Ames"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Six guns west of Willis Church Road; the episode credits the battery with firing 1,392 rounds"}]]}],["$","div","Reinforcing corps commanders-Maj. Gens. Samuel P. Heintzelman and Edwin V. Sumner",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Reinforcing corps commanders"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gens. Samuel P. Heintzelman and Edwin V. Sumner"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Elements of their commands moved forward to support the established line late in the battle"}]]}]]]}],["$","section","Confederacy",{"className":"command-column #9a3d32","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Confederacy"}],[["$","div","Army of Northern Virginia-Gen. Robert E. Lee",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of Northern Virginia"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Gen. Robert E. Lee"}],"$L1f"]}],"$L20","$L21","$L22","$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26"]]}]]}],"$L27"]]]}]
1a:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L28","/images/ep167-malvern-hill-waud.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep167-malvern-hill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of United States infantry and artillery at Malvern Hill.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud made this drawing on July 1, 1862. Its period title identifies the Federal victory over forces led by Magruder and Jackson.","name":"Alfred Waud drawing of United States infantry and artillery at Malvern Hill.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L28","/images/ep167-malvern-hill-currier-ives.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep167-malvern-hill-currier-ives.jpg","alt":"Hand-colored Currier and Ives lithograph depicting the Battle of Malvern Hill.","caption":"Currier and Ives published this hand-colored battle lithograph. It is a popular print rather than a literal reconstruction of troop positions.","name":"Hand-colored Currier and Ives lithograph depicting the Battle of Malvern Hill.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L28","/images/ep167-magruder.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep167-magruder.jpg","alt":"Full-length photographic portrait of Confederate general John B. Magruder in uniform.","caption":"John B. Magruder organized and directed most of the late Confederate infantry attacks after receiving Lee’s orders.","name":"Full-length photographic portrait of Confederate general John B. Magruder in uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L28","/images/ep167-adelbert-ames.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep167-adelbert-ames.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of United States officer Adelbert Ames.","caption":"At Malvern Hill, Lt. Adelbert Ames commanded Battery A, 5th U.S. Artillery. The episode credits its six guns with 1,392 rounds fired.","name":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of United States officer Adelbert Ames.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L28","/images/ep167-harrisons-landing-waud.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep167-harrisons-landing-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of the United States defensive line at Harrison’s Landing.","caption":"Waud drew artillery and fortifications at Harrison’s Landing in July or August 1862 after the Army of the Potomac established its James River base.","name":"Alfred Waud drawing of the United States defensive line at Harrison’s Landing.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}]]}]
